  "unit": "Unit 18 - Angles",
  "description": "Comprehensive lesson covering Unit 18 - Angles for SAT Math preparation.",
  "duration": 45,
  "content": "<p>In a triangle, the <strong>exterior angle</strong> is equal to the sum of the two non-adjacent interior angles.</p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>Exterior&#x000A0;Angle</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>Remote&#x000A0;Interior&#x000A0;Angle&#x000A0;1</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mtext>Remote&#x000A0;Interior&#x000A0;Angle&#x000A0;2</mtext></mrow></math>\n\n<p><strong>Example 1</strong><br />\nIn a triangle, one exterior angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. If one remote interior angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, the other is:  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>80</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>\n\n<hr />\n\n<p>When a transversal cuts two parallel lines:  </p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Corresponding angles</strong> are equal.  </li>\n<li><strong>Alternate interior angles</strong> are equal.  </li>\n<li><strong>Same-side interior angles</strong> are supplementary.  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 2</strong><br />\nIf one angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, then the corresponding angle is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<h3>Interior Angle Sum</h3>\n<p>For an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-sided polygon:  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>Sum&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;interior&#x000A0;angles</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>\n\n<h3>Each Interior Angle (regular polygon)</h3>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n\n<h3>Each Exterior Angle (regular polygon)</h3>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>360</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>\n\n<p><strong>Example 3</strong><br />\nA hexagon (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>):<br />\n- Interior angle sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>720</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math><br />\n- Each interior angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>720</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complementary angles</strong>: sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>  </li>\n<li><strong>Supplementary angles</strong>: sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>  </li>\n<li><strong>Vertical angles</strong>: equal  </li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Example 4</strong><br />\nIf two supplementary angles are in ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>:</mi><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>, find them.  </p>\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>180</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>180</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>\n\n<p>Angles = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mn>140</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<ul>\n<li>Always check for parallel lines → look for equal angles.  </li>\n<li>For polygons, memorize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><msup><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </li>\n<li>Exterior angle theorem is very common.  </li>\n<li>Watch out for “trick” wording with supplements/complements.  </li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) In a triangle, one angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math> and another is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Find the exterior angle adjacent to the third angle.<br />\n2) Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. If one angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>, find its alternate interior angle.<br />\n3) Find the sum of interior angles of a decagon.<br />\n4) Find each interior angle of a regular octagon.<br />\n5) Two angles are complementary. One is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the other is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<hr />\n\n<p>1) Third angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>70</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>. Exterior angle = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>70</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>110</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>2) Alternate interior angle = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>75</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>3) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>1440</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>4) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>&#x000D7;</mi><mn>180</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>108</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<p>5) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></math>.  </p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">\n</code></pre>",
